tting them back together again.\" [2181] \"From all the evidence now, it can be determined that Queen Farajka is still in contact with her ally, the Victror King. It is very likely that the original plan was his idea. It is now known that she has been maintaining contact with the Victror King in the Hesse-Slavonia palace using coded messages. For safety, all the letters were written in English and signed with the name of a British diplomat's wife who was stationed at the embassy at the time. Even if questioned, the lady would deny it, and no one would believe her, as the letters were written by a woman who knew she was in the wrong and wrote them to her lover. She used your name, Mrs. Reve.\" [2182] \"I know,\" Virginia said. Her face flushed with red and white. \"So that's what those letters were about! I've been wondering about it.\" [2183] \"What a despicable scheme,\" Bill said. [2184] \"The letters were addressed to Captain O'Neill's residence in Paris, and it was only after the strange discovery that the true purpose of the letters became clear. After the King and Queen were assassinated, many jewels from the royal family fell into the hands of the rioting crowd and were transported to Paris. It was later discovered that 90% of them had been switched. Please note that some of the jewels in the Hesse-Slavonia palace were of great value. It is clear that Anne Louise Millet, after becoming queen, continued her old trade.\" [2185] \"Now you know the progress we have made in this matter. King Nicholas IV and Queen Farajka came to visit Britain and were the guests of the then Foreign Minister, the late Earl of Chatham. Although Hesse-Slavonia is a small country, it cannot be ignored, and Queen Farajka was naturally invited to Chimney House. Among the guests was a royal nobleman who was also a professional thief. It is certain that the counterfeit, which could pass for the real thing, was made by the Victror King. Moreover, the whole plan was\n[2186] \"Then what happened?\" Virginia asked. [2187] \"We sealed the information,\" Chief Constable Bately said succinctly. [2188] \"It has not been made public to this day. We have done everything we can privately, much more than you can imagine. We have used many remarkable methods. What I can tell you is that Queen Faraja did not take the jewel out of Britain. Yes, the queen hid it somewhere, but we have not found out where. But I think,\" Chief Constable Bately looked around, \"it may be in this room.\" [2189] Anthony jumped up. [2190] \"What? Still here after all these years?\" He exclaimed in disbelief. [2191] \"Impossible.\" [2192] \"Sir, you do not understand the special circumstances at the time,\" the Frenchman replied quickly, \"Just two weeks later, the Hersovsky Revolution broke out, and the King and Queen were killed. At the same time, Captain O'Neill was arrested in Paris and sentenced to prison for a minor offense. We hoped to find those secret letters in his house, but it seems that they were stolen by Hersovsky's emissaries. The man appeared in Hersovsky before the revolution and then disappeared.\" [2193] \"He may have fled abroad,\" Anthony said thoughtfully. [2194] \"He may have gone to Africa, but that package must be in his hands, which is a gold mine for him. It's strange, I don't know what happened. The man seems to be named Pedro or something.\" [2195] He saw Sheriff Bartlett glance at him without expression and smiled. [2196] \"Bartlett, I'm not a seer.\" He said, \"Although it sounds plausible. I'll be open and honest later.\" [2197] \"There's one thing you haven't mentioned,\" Virginia said, \"What does this have to do with the memoir? It must be related, right?\" [2198] \"Madam, you're very direct.\" Lemont praised. [2199] \"Yes, there is a connection. At that time, the Count of Stapleton was also at the Chimney House.\" [2200] \"So he might have known?\" [2201] \"Definitely.\" [2202] \"Of course,\" Bartle said. \"If he mentioned it in that precious memoir, it would be a disaster, especially considering how long the whole thing has been covered up.\" [2203] Anthony lit a cigarette. [2204] \"The memoir couldn't have any clues about the hiding place of the gem, could it?\" he asked. [2205] \"It's unlikely,\" Bartle said decisively. \"He wouldn't get close to the queen, he opposed that marriage vehemently. She wouldn't confide in him.\" [2206] \"I didn't mean that,\" said Anthony. \"But it's said that he's a cunning old fox who might have found out where she hid her jewels when she wasn't looking. What do you think he would do then?\" [2207] \"Stay the same,\" Bartlett said after a moment's thought. [2208] \"I agree,\" said the Frenchman. \"It was a tricky situation back then. Anonymous return of the jewels was difficult. Knowing its location would give him great power, and that strange old man was always eager for power. He could not only control the queen but also have a bargaining chip at any time. The secrets he possessed were not just one; he collected secrets like some people collected rare porcelain. It was said that he had boasted a few times before his death that when he was in a good mood, he would reveal all he knew; at least once, he announced that he would stir up a storm in his memoir. [2209] \"Still, there's no reason to assume he knows this secret,\" said Bartlett. [2210] \"I apologize,\" said Anthony calmly. \"That's his own words.\" [2211] \"What?\" [2212] The two detectives stared at him in disbelief. [2213] \"McGrath gave me the manuscript and asked me to bring it to England,\" Anthony continued. \"He told me about his encounter with the Count of Stapleton in Paris. McGrath had saved him from a group of thugs, and while he was drunk, he said two interesting things: one was that he knew the location of the Light of Heaven diamond, which my friends didn't pay much attention to; the other was that the thugs who attacked him were from the court of Victor King. These two pieces of information together are worth pondering.\" [2214] \"Good heavens,\" Batters exclaimed, \"we must ponder this! This changes the meaning of Michael King's murder.\" [2215] \"Victor King never kills,\" the Frenchman reminded him. [2216] \"What if he was discovered while looking for jewels?\" [2217] \"So, he's in England now?\" [2218] \"You said he was released months ago. Didn't you follow him?\" [2219] The French detective's face showed a slightly guilty smile. [2220] \"We tried to follow him, but he was too experienced and escaped immediately. Of course, we thought he would go directly to England, but he didn't. He went... do you think it could be...\"\n[2221] \"Where?\" Anthony said. [2222] He stared intently at the French detective, idly playing with a box of matches in his hand. [2223] \"The United States, the United States of America.\" [2224] \"What?\" [2225] Anthony's tone was filled with astonishment. [2226] \"Yes. Do you think he'll call himself something different this time? What will he do there? Prince Nikolai of Hesse-Lobenstein.\" [2227] The matchbox fell from Anthony's hand to the ground, and Balthasar's surprise matched his. [2228] \"Impossible!\" [2229] \"That's right. You'll see the news in the morning. It's the biggest fraud case! Everyone knows that Prince Nikolai was rumored to have died in the Congo a few years ago. This fellow, Prince Viktor, seized the opportunity because it's hard to prove such a death.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[287] \"It could be even worse,\" he continued, taking a breath. \"Suppose, just suppose, that the unfortunate disappearance incident is dug up again. You know what I'm talking about, right?\" [288] The Marquess of Carrington stared at him intently. [289] \"I don't understand. What disappearance incident?\" [290] \"You must have heard about it. It happened when they were all at the chimney villa. Henry was very worried, and his career was almost ruined because of that incident.\" [291] \"This sounds so intriguing,\" said the Marquess of Carrington. \"Who or what disappeared?\" [292] Luo Maximus leaned forward, bringing his mouth close to the Marquess's ear. The Marquess stepped back hastily. \"Oh, don't whisper in my ear like that.\" [293] \"Did you hear that?\" [294] \"Yes, I heard it,\" the Marquess of Carrington said with disgust. \"I remember hearing some strange rumors at the time. I've always wondered who could have done it, and that thing hasn't been found yet?\" [295] \"No, it hasn't. We'll handle this matter with utmost caution and won't let any wind of it get out. The Earl of Stapleton was also present, and although he didn't know much about it, he did know a little. If he held a grudge and made that incident public, think about the scandal it would cause and the far-reaching impact it would have. Everyone would ask why we're keeping it under wraps.\" [296] \"Of course they would,\" said the Marquess of Carringham with enthusiasm. [297] Lord Roxburgh's voice rose a few octaves as he struggled to control himself. [298] \"I must keep my cool,\" he muttered. \"I must keep my cool. What if he had no ill intentions and went to all this trouble to send the manuscript to London?\" [299] \"That is quite strange. Are you sure all of this is true?\" [300] \"Absolutely true. We have agents in Paris. The memoir was secretly moved a few weeks before his death.\" [301] \"It does seem there is more to it,\" said the Marquess of Carrington, regaining some of his earlier enthusiasm. [302] \"We found that the manuscripts were sent to a man named Jimmy, or James, a Canadian now living in Africa.\" [303] \"This is quite elaborate,\" said the Marquess of Carrington, clearly pleased. [304] \"James McGregor will arrive on the Grangewood tomorrow, Thursday.\" [305] \"What do you plan to do?\" [306] \"We must find him immediately and explain the serious consequences of this. We should ask him to delay the publication of the memoir at least for a month, and we must edit it carefully.\" [307] \"What if he says 'No, sir!' or 'Go to hell!'?\" said the Earl of Carringham. [308] \"I'm afraid that's what he might say.\" Lord Runciman said calmly. \"So, I suddenly thought, inviting him to the Chimney House might be a good idea. He would be delighted to meet the Prince Michael, and it would be much easier to deal with him.\" [309] \"I disagree.\" said the Earl of Carringham hastily. \"I can't get along with Canadians, especially those who have lived in Africa.\" [310] \"He might be a very good person, a promising candidate.\" [311] \"I refuse, Romax. I firmly oppose, find someone else to deal with him.\" [312] \"I have a brilliant idea,\" said Roxane, \"a woman! Find a woman to deal with him, tell him a little, but not too much. Women can cleverly make him understand the situation without angering him. I must admit that I am not in favor of women in politics, but even though Saint Stephen's Cathedral is now a complete ruin, women can still play a magical role in their own domains. Look at Henry's wife, she was a great help to him. Maria is truly remarkable, an exceptional and perfect political wife.\" [313] \"Are you going to invite Masha to the party?\" asked the Marquess of Carrington, looking a little pale at the mention of his formidable sister-in-law. [314] \"No, no, you misunderstood. I was just giving an example of a woman's influence. I still suggest finding a young, attractive, beautiful, and intelligent woman.\" [315] \"Bendel is no good either, she can't help at all, she's just a fanatical socialist. If you tell her that, she'll laugh herself silly.\" [316] \"I haven't considered Miss Elinor. Your daughter is very charming, but she's just a child. We need a clever, calm, and versatile girl. I have a perfect alternative, my cousin Virginia.\" [317] \"Mrs. Rue?\" the Earl of Carringham smiled, starting to think the party might be interesting after all. \"That's a great suggestion, Romax. She's the most attractive woman in London.\" [318] \"She's also well-versed in Hesse-Slav. You should remember, her husband worked in the embassy there. And, like you said, she has a lot of personal charm.\" [319] \"A real beauty,\" the Earl murmured. [320] \"Then it's settled.\" [321] Mr. Roxmax finally released his grip on the Earl of Carringham's collar, allowing the Earl to quickly escape. [322] \"Goodbye! Roxanne! You take care of it, please.\" [323] Carterham got into a taxi, he was really sick of George Romex. His red and swollen face, heavy breathing, and bulging serious blue eyes annoyed him. He sighed thinking about the weekend gathering. It was really annoying, very annoying! Then he thought of Virginia Ruff, and his mood brightened up a bit. [324] \"What a human marvel,\" he muttered to himself, \"what a human marvel.\" [325] Chapter 4: The Enchanting Lady\n[326] George Roxton returned directly to Whitehall [1]. As he entered the luxurious building, a flurry of footsteps echoed. It was here that he handled various state affairs. [327] At this time, Bill Everleigh was diligently filing various documents, but the large sofa chair by the window still retained the residual warmth from someone who had just sat there. [328] Bill Everleigh was a likable young man, appearing to be in his mid-twenties, with a robust build and somewhat clumsy movements. Although his appearance was unattractive, he was not unpleasant to look at. His teeth were white and even, and his brown eyes were sincere. [329] \"Has Richardson's report arrived yet?\" [330] \"No, I'll ask him.\" [331] \"No need. Any messages on the phone?\" [332] \"These matters are mainly handled by Miss Oscar, and Mr. Issacson asked if you could have lunch with him at the Savoy Hotel tomorrow.\" [333] \"Have Miss Oscar check my schedule. If there are no other appointments, have her call and accept.\" [334] \"Yes, sir.\" [335] \"Oh, Everly, also call and check Mrs. Ruff's telephone number at 487 Pound Street for me.\" [336] \"Will do.\" Bill picked up the phone book, pretended to look down the list under the surname column, and then slammed the phone book shut and walked over to the desk. He put his hand on the phone receiver, as if suddenly remembering something, and hesitated for a moment. [337] \"Sir, I just remembered that Mrs. Rue's phone line is out. I called her a little while ago.\" [338] George Romax frowned. [339] \"Frustrating,\" he said. \"Very frustrating.\" He tapped the table lightly, hesitating. [340] \"If there's something important, I can go over there in a taxi right now. She should be home.\" [341] George Romax pondered for a moment, with Bill waiting anxiously by his side. If the answer was affirmative, he would rush out immediately. [342] \"Perhaps this is the best course of action,\" Romax finally said. \"You go in a taxi and ask Mrs. Ruff if she'll be home this afternoon at four o'clock. Tell her I have something very important to discuss with her.\" [343] \"Yes, sir.\" Bill grabbed his hat and quickly left. [344] Ten minutes later, a taxi dropped him off at 487 Pound Street. He rang the doorbell and knocked heavily on the door knocker. A serious-looking servant opened the door, and Bill nodded to her, recognizing her as an acquaintance. [345] \"Good morning, Chivers. Is Mrs. Ruff at home?\" [346] \"She is preparing to leave.\" [347] \"Bill, is that you?\" A voice came down the staircase, \"I knew it was you as soon as I heard the pounding on the door. Come up.\" [348] Bill looked up and saw a smiling face peering down at him, a face that always had a special charm that made anyone who saw it, including Bill, lose their composure. He bounded up the stairs two steps at a time, taking the hand that Virginia extended to him. [349] \"Hi, Virginia!\" [350] \"Hello, Bill!\" [351] Attraction is a mysterious thing: there are so many young women in the world, some even more beautiful than Virginia Ruef, and even if they say \"Hello, Bill\" with the same tone, it won't have any special effect. But when Virginia says those simple words, Bill feels spellbound. [352] Virginia was twenty-seven years old, tall and slender with a perfectly proportioned figure that was like poetry. Her hair was a pure copper, with a golden undertone and a hint of green. Her chin was small and determined, her nose cute, and her eyes blue. When she opened her eyelids, they glowed with a deep blue light, and her eyes sparkled. Her lips were always slightly upturned, giving her a wonderful, indescribable \"Venusian air\" that was full of expression. She exuded an aura of vitality, and wherever she went, she was the center of attention, and no one could ignore her charm. [353] She pulled Bill into the small living room, which was decorated in pale purple, green, and yellow, giving the impression of a field of daffodils hiding in the grass. [354] \"Bill, dear,\" Virginia said. \"I'm sure the State Department misses you. They can't function without you, can they?\" [355] \"I'm here to deliver a message from Mr. Kodes,\" Bill said disrespectfully, referring to his boss by his first name. [356] \"Oh, and if he asks, tell him your phone was broken this morning.\" [357] \"But it wasn't.\" [358] \"I know, but I told him it was.\" [359] \"Why? What's the story behind this diplomatic language?\" [360] Bill gives her a reproachful look. [361] \"Only then could I come and see you.\" [362] \"Oh, darling, you're so sweet!\" [363] \"Chivvers just said you were about to go out.\" [364] \"Yes, I'm going to Sloane Street. They've just brought out a lovely new girdle.\" [365] \"Girdle?\" [366] \"Yes, a garter\u2014belt\u2014a tight band worn around the hips.\" [367] \"What a shame! Talking about underwear with a young man like that. It's not very elegant.\" [368] \"But the hips aren't vulgar. Everyone has hips, but we poor women have to go to great lengths to pretend we don't. The garter belt is made of red rubber and goes up to just above the knee. It's a real pain to walk in.\" [369] \"That's terrible!\" said Bill. \"Why would you wear that?\" [370] \"Because women are willing to suffer for their figure. Let's not talk about garter belts anymore. What did George say?\" [371] \"He asked if you'll be home at 4 pm today.\" [372] \"No, I'm going to Larnaca. Why would he want to make such a formal visit? Is he going to propose to me?\" [373] \"If he does, I wouldn't be surprised.\" [374] \"If that's the case, tell him I prefer it when men propose out of the blue, spontaneously.\" [375] \"Like me?\" [376] \"You're not spontaneous, you're habitual.\" [377] \"Virginia, will you\u2026?\" [378] \"Alright, alright, Bill, don't be silly in the morning. You should treat me like a middle-aged woman with maternal instincts. I'll remember your feelings. \" [379] \"Virginia, I really love you.\" [380] \"I know, Bill, I know. I just like feeling loved. Am I bad for that? I hope all the good men in the world like me.\" [381] \"There are enough people who love you already.\" Bill said dejectedly. [382] \"But I don't want George to like me. I don't think he will either, he's married to his career. What else did he say?\" [383] \"He just said he had something important to discuss with you.\" [384] \"I'm getting more and more interested. There aren't many things that make George feel important. I'm not going to Rainerch today, I can go anytime. Tell George I'll be waiting for him at 4 o'clock.\" [385] Bill checked his wristwatch. [386] \"It's a pity to go back before lunch. Let's go out for some food, Virginia.\" [387] \"I'm going out for lunch.\" [388] \"It's okay, just stay with me today. Cancel all other arrangements.\" [389] \"Okay,\" Virginia smiled at him. [390] \"Virginia, you're so nice. You actually like me, don't you? You like me more than others, don't you?\" [391] \"Bill, I really like you. If I had to marry someone, like in a book where a bad guy says to me, 'You have to marry someone, or I'll torture you little by little,' then I'd definitely choose you. I mean it. I'd say, 'Give me Bill!'\" [392] \"So\u2026\"\n[393] \"But I don't have to marry someone. I enjoy being a bad widow.\" [394] \"Married to me, you can still do whatever you want, go anywhere, do anything, without worrying about me.\" [395] \"Bill, you don't understand me. I'm the kind of person who would be passionate if I got married.\" [396] Bill sighed heavily. [397] \"I feel like I'm going to commit suicide someday.\" He grumbled dejectedly. [398] \"You won't. You'll go out to dinner with a pretty girl, like you did the other night.\" [399] Everly was a bit taken aback. [400] \"You mean Dorothy Kirkpatrick, the girl at the 'Hook and Button Restaurant'? Yeah, she's a nice girl, proper, and there's nothing wrong with her.\" [401] \"Of course not. I'm happy when you're happy, but don't pretend to be heartbroken.\" [402] Eversley regained some of his self-esteem. [403] \"Virginia, you don't understand,\" he said sternly, \"men... \"\n[404] \"They all like polygamy! I understand. Sometimes I wonder if I'm in favor of polyandry. If you really love me, take me to lunch right now.\" [405] ***\n[406] Whitehall is a street in the City of London that connects the House of Commons and Downing Street. Along this street and nearby are located the Ministry of Defence, the Foreign and Commonwealth Office, the Home Office, and the Admiralty, among other British government departments. Therefore, \"Whitehall\" is often used as a synonym for the British civil service. [407] Chapter 5: The First Night in London\n[408] Even the most meticulous plans have flaws. George Romilly made a mistake that created a fatal flaw in his plan, and Bill was that flaw. [409] Bill was a good kid who excelled in cricket and golf. He was elegant in his manner and amiable in his disposition. He got his job at the Foreign Office through his charm rather than his intellect, and he was well-suited for the position. His job required little responsibility and no brainpower, and he was like a little dog at George's side. His task was to be at George's beck and call, to run errands, and to make himself useful in any way he could. Bill did all of this diligently. When George was away, Bill would flop into the largest sofa chair and comfortably read the sports news, a tradition that had been going on for a long time. [410] George had grown accustomed to sending Bill on errands, and this time he sent Bill to the office of the Whitehall Shipping Company to inquire about the arrival time of the Granaanburgh. Like many well-bred British young men, Bill spoke with a pleasant voice but with some difficulty in pronunciation. The words \"Granaanburgh\" that he uttered would have made any speechmaster frown and sound awkward and out of place. The clerk at the shipping company heard \"Carnfrae.\" [411] The clerk said that Carnfrae would arrive on the following Thursday, and Bill thanked him before leaving. George Romilly received the message and adjusted his schedule accordingly. He had no knowledge of the Union Castle Line's sailing schedule and assumed that Jimmy McGlashan would arrive on Thursday. [412] Therefore, on Tuesday morning of that week, when he kept the Earl of Carringham waiting on the club steps, he was still unaware that the Granada had docked at Southampton the previous afternoon. At 2 pm that afternoon, Anthony Kede, a traveler with the name Jimmy Romilly, stepped out of the cabin and hailed a taxi at Waterloo Station. After a moment's thought, he instructed the driver to head to the Britz Hotel. [413] \"It's all right to relax now,\" Anthony said, gazing out the window with interest. [414] It had been fourteen years since he had last left London. [415] After checking into the hotel, he went for a walk along the riverbank. It was a joyous feeling to be back in London, although everything had changed. The Blackfriars Bridge area used to be a small restaurant where he and his friends often dined. He was a socialist back then, always wearing a red scarf. He was so young at the time. [416] He turned around and began to retrace his steps back to the hotel. Just as he was crossing the street, a man bumped into him full force, almost causing him to fall. Both men steadied themselves, and the man, who was of short stature with a muscular build, began to mutter an apology while staring intently at Anthony's face. He had the appearance of a typical working-class man, and seemed to be a foreigner. [417] Anthony was pondering the man's inquisitive gaze as he walked back. It was rather peculiar! Perhaps it was the man's tanned complexion that stood out among the pale Londoners. He returned to his room and, on a whim, walked up to the mirror to examine himself closely.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:22 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nLike the dining room, this room was lined with oak paneling and had a spacious gallery. At the end of the hallway, two people appeared. [1683] \"Ah,\" said Mr. Fitch, \"our gracious host has arrived.\" [1684] His description of the Earl of Carrington was so ridiculous that Anthony couldn't help but turn his head so that others wouldn't see him laughing. [1685] \"The lady with him,\" the American continued, \"I saw last night, but I can't remember her name. She's very intelligent, very intelligent.\" [1686] The woman with the Earl of Carton was Virginia Reeve. [1687] Anthony had always known that he would meet her, but he had no idea how to react. He handed the floor to Virginia. Although he knew she would remain composed, he had no idea what she would say. Of course, he would soon find out. [1688] \"Ah, Mr. Kidd,\" Virginia said, extending her hands to him, \"you've finally arrived.\" [1689] \"Dear Mrs. Reeve,\" the Earl of Carton said, \"I didn't know that Mr. Kidd was your friend.\" [1690] \"An old friend,\" Virginia smiled at Anthony, her eyes twinkling mischievously. [1691] \"I met him by chance in London yesterday, and I told him I was coming here.\" [1692] Anthony gave her a hint. [1693] \"I've explained to Mrs. Reeve,\" he said, \"that the invitation wasn't for me, so I had to decline your kind offer. I couldn't very well impersonate someone else to deceive you.\" [1694] \"Well, well, old chap,\" Cathcart said, \"all that is now behind us. I'll have someone go to the Cricketer's Inn to fetch your luggage.\" [1695] \"Thank you for your generosity, Cathcart, but...\n[1696] \"You must come to the Chimney House, of course, Mr. Kidd,\" said Virginia gently. \"You can't stay in that inn. It's simply not decent.\" [1697] \"You must come, Mr. Kidd,\" said Virginia gently. [1698] Anthony realized that the situation had changed. Virginia had paved the way for him, and he was no longer an unknown stranger. Her position was indisputable, so her vouching for him would be taken for granted. He thought of the gun hidden in the willow grove and smiled to himself. [1699] \"I will have someone fetch your luggage,\" said the Earl of Chatham to Anthony. \"I'm afraid we won't be able to go hunting now. It's a pity. I don't know what to do about Essex. It's too bad.\" [1700] The Earl of Chatham sighed sadly. [1701] \"Well, it's settled then,\" Virginia said. \"You can be of use now, Mr. Kidd. Let's go for a walk by the lake. It's far from the crime scene and relatively peaceful. It's unfortunate that the Earl of Carringham's estate has been involved in a murder case, but it's all George's fault, he invited those people.\" [1702] \"Ah,\" the Earl of Carringham said, \"I shouldn't have listened to him!\" [1703] He looked crestfallen, like a strong man being let down by his team. [1704] \"George always gets his way,\" Virginia said. \"He always pulls you in and doesn't let you get away. I'm thinking of making a detachable collar and applying for a patent.\" [1705] \"I wish you luck,\" the host chuckled. \"I'm glad you're here. I need support.\" [1706] \"Thank you, Duke of Carringham,\" said Anthony. \"Especially for a suspicious character like me. But, living here could make the police chief's job easier.\" [1707] \"In what way, sir?\" asked the police chief. [1708] \"Monitoring me won't be too difficult,\" Anthony whispered. [1709] The police chief's eyes lit up, knowing that Anthony's words were exactly what he wanted to hear. [1710] * * *\n[1711] [1] Albrecht D\u00fcrer (c. 1497-1543), a German painter, was one of the most famous painters of the Renaissance period. [1712] [2] Anthony van Dyck (1599-1641), a Flemish Baroque painter. [1713] [3] Diego Vel\u00e1zquez (1599-1660), one of the greatest writers of the post-Renaissance period in Spain. [1714] Chapter 16: Classroom Tea\n[1715] Anthony returned to the platform, feeling in his heart that only the center of the lake was the only safe place for a secret conversation. [1716] There was a loud rumble of gongs coming from inside the house, and Trudwell emerged from the side door, solemnly addressing the Earl:\n[1717] \"My lord, lunch is ready.\" [1718] \"Ah,\" said the Earl of Carrham, slightly perking up, \"let's have lunch.\" [1719] Suddenly, two lively little girls came running out of the house, one twelve and the other ten. Although Bandler had said their names were Daisy and Dee, everyone called them Giggles and Sparkles. They jumped around in a war dance until Bandler appeared and quieted them down. [1720] \"Where's the teacher?\" she asked. [1721] \"She has a headache, a headache, a headache!\" Shimmer sang loudly. [1722] \"Wow!\" Giggles exclaimed. [1723] The Earl of Carrickham had already invited most of the guests into the house, and now he took Anthony's arm in one hand. [1724] \"Come with me to my study,\" he whispered. \"There are some special things there.\" [1725] The Earl of Carrickham slipped into his study, a secretive manner that did not resemble the owner of the villa at all, but rather a thief. Then, he opened a cabinet and took out several bottles of wine. [1726] \"I get thirsty when I talk to foreigners,\" he explained apologetically. \"I don't know why.\" [1727] At that moment, there was a knock on the door, and Virginia poked her head in. [1728] \"Do you have any cocktails?\" she asked. [1729] \"Of course,\" the Earl of Carrington said enthusiastically. \"Come in.\" [1730] The next few minutes were spent with everyone exchanging drinks and having a good time. [1731] \"I was just dying for that,\" the Duke of Cambridge sighed, putting his glass back on the table, \"As I said, I find talking to foreigners particularly exhausting, probably because they're too polite. Let's go have lunch.\" [1732] He led everyone to the dining room, and Virginia took Anthony's arm, pulling him slightly to the side. [1733] \"I did something big today,\" she whispered, \"I convinced the Duke of Cambridge to take me to see the body.\" [1734] \"How was it?\" Anthony asked eagerly. [1735] The truth of his thoughts was about to be revealed. [1736] Virginia shook her head. [1737] \"You're wrong,\" she whispered. \"It was Michael Prince, no doubt about it.\" [1738] \"Oh,\" Anthony felt very disappointed. [1739] \"The tutor's headache,\" he said loudly, with an annoyed tone. [1740] \"What does that have to do with this?\" [1741] \"Maybe not, but I still want to see her. You know, I've found out that the person living in the second room from the beginning is her, the one I saw with the light on last night.\" [1742] \"That's interesting.\" [1743] \"Maybe it doesn't have anything to do with it, but I still want to see her before it gets dark.\" [1744] Lunch was eaten with some discomfort for everyone. Even Bunder's lively and cheerful demeanor could not dispel the somber atmosphere of the mixed gathering. The baron and the duke of Andrews appeared stiff and solemn, as if dining in a mausoleum; the Earl of Carrington was also lifeless and gloomy. [1745] Bill Everly always looked hungrily at Virginia; George felt awkward in his position and tried to engage the baron and Mr. Isakov in conversation. [1746] The twins were lost in the joy of the commotion at home and had to be constantly restrained. Mr. Hale Fisk was slowly chewing his food and occasionally saying a few bland words in his usual slow, drawling manner. Sheriff Butler was nowhere to be seen, and no one knew his whereabouts. [1747] \"Thank God, we're done,\" Bunder muttered to Anthony as they left the table. \"George is going to bring the foreign delegation to the monastery this afternoon to discuss national affairs.\" [1748] \"That should help to lighten the mood,\" Anthony said approvingly. [1749] \"I don't mind the American,\" Bunder continued. \"He can have a quiet afternoon with his father talking about rare books.\" [1750] The American bowed deeply to her. [1751] \"You're too kind, Miss Erin,\" he said. [1752] \"Mr. Fischer has had a quiet morning,\" Anthony said. [1753] Mr. Fischer glanced at him quickly. [1754] \"Did you see me hiding in there? Sometimes, the only motto for a person who likes solitude is 'stay away from the crazy crowd.'\" [1755] \"Yes, there is,\" Anthony said. [1756] \"That bald guy is related to the family, too?\" [1757] \"Probably.\" [1758] \"These Central European countries are too chaotic,\" said Mr. Fitch. \"Is it true that the victim was a prince?\" [1759] \"He's staying here under the name of Count Stanislaus,\" Anthony said evasively. [1760] Mr. Fitch made no reply, but exclaimed mysteriously, \"My goodness!\" [1761] He then fell silent for a moment before speaking again. [1762] \"Is your constable, Batters or something, a crack shot?\" [1763] \"Scotland Yard thinks so,\" Anthony said coldly. [1764] \"I find him a bit conservative,\" Mr. Fitch said, \"lacking in initiative. What's the point of not allowing anyone to leave the house? That's all he seems to have done.\" [1765] He gla